# Verify signed webhooks in a Plug pipeline

This notebook builds a webhook receiver with `Plug.Router` and RequestSeal's Plug integration, then sends it signed, unsigned, and altered requests through `Plug.Test`. Phoenix endpoints use the same plugs. The requests go straight into the pipeline in this process; no server, port, or network is involved beyond the Hex install.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:request_seal, "~> 0.5.1"}, {:plug, "~> 1.20.3"}])
```

## Configure the sender and the policy

The sender holds the private key. The receiver's policy trusts only the sender's public key and requires the body digest to be signed.

```elixir
{_public_bytes, seed} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)
{:ok, sender} = RequestSeal.Custody.Local.new("ed25519", {:ed25519, seed})
{:ok, sender_key} = RequestSeal.Custody.public_key(sender)

components = ~s[("@method" "@scheme" "@authority" "@path" "content-digest")]

{:ok, policy} =
  RequestSeal.Policy.new(%{
    algorithms: ["ed25519"],
    components: components,
    key_resolver: fn
      %{keyid: "orders-service"} -> {:ok, %{algorithm: "ed25519", key: sender_key}}
      _ -> :error
    end,
    freshness: %{
      clock: fn -> System.system_time(:second) end,
      max_age: 60,
      skew: 5,
      require_expires: true
    },
    content: %{kind: :content, algorithms: ["sha-256"], section: :headers},
    replay: :not_required
  })

Application.put_env(:webhook_notebook, :signature_policy, policy)
IO.puts("Policy configured")
```

## Build the receiver

`RequestSeal.Plug.Capture` keeps the exact body bytes before `Plug.Parsers` consumes them, and `Plug.Parsers` reads through Capture's body reader. `RequestSeal.Plug.Verify` then rejects anything that fails the policy before the route runs. Elixir's built-in `JSON` module decodes the body.

```elixir
defmodule ShipmentWebhook do
  use Plug.Router

  plug(RequestSeal.Plug.Capture,
    origin: :connection,
    max_body_bytes: 65_536,
    read_timeout: 5_000
  )

  plug(Plug.Parsers,
    parsers: [:json],
    pass: ["*/*"],
    json_decoder: JSON,
    body_reader: {RequestSeal.Plug.Capture, :read_body, []}
  )

  plug(RequestSeal.Plug.Verify,
    policy: {Application, :fetch_env!, [:webhook_notebook, :signature_policy]},
    label: "sig",
    on_reject: {:halt, 401}
  )

  plug(:match)
  plug(:dispatch)

  post "/hooks/shipments" do
    {:ok, verified} = RequestSeal.Plug.verification(conn)
    send_resp(conn, 200, "#{verified.signature.crypto}: #{conn.body_params["status"]}")
  end

  match _ do
    send_resp(conn, 404, "not found")
  end
end

IO.puts("Receiver compiled")
```

## Send a signed request

The sender signs with the core API. Its headers, including `Content-Digest`, `Signature-Input`, and `Signature`, go onto a `Plug.Test` connection with the same body.

```elixir
url = "https://hooks.example.com/hooks/shipments"
event = ~s({"shipment":"SHP-2207","status":"delivered"})

{:ok, request} =
  RequestSeal.Message.request("POST", url, [{"content-type", "application/json"}], event,
    digest: ["sha-256"]
  )

{:ok, signed} =
  RequestSeal.sign(
    request,
    %{
      label: "sig",
      algorithm: "ed25519",
      components: components,
      expires_in: 60,
      keyid: "orders-service"
    },
    sender
  )

deliver = fn headers, body ->
  conn =
    Enum.reduce(headers, Plug.Test.conn(:post, url, body), fn {name, value}, conn ->
      Plug.Conn.put_req_header(conn, String.downcase(name), value)
    end)

  ShipmentWebhook.call(conn, ShipmentWebhook.init([]))
end

signed_headers = Enum.map(signed.fields, &{&1.name, &1.value})
accepted = deliver.(signed_headers, event)

200 = accepted.status
"valid: delivered" = accepted.resp_body
IO.puts("Signed request: #{accepted.status} #{accepted.resp_body}")
```

## Reject unsigned and altered requests

```elixir
unsigned = deliver.([{"content-type", "application/json"}], event)
401 = unsigned.status

altered = deliver.(signed_headers, ~s({"shipment":"SHP-2207","status":"returned"}))
401 = altered.status

# No verification result is available for a rejected request.
:error = RequestSeal.Plug.verification(altered)

# Verify keeps the bounded rejection in private connection state for logging.
{:error, error} = altered.private.request_seal.verification
:request_rejected = error.reason
%{reason: :digest_mismatch, layer: :content} = error.source

IO.puts("Unsigned: #{unsigned.status}; altered body: #{altered.status}")
```

`RequestSeal.Plug.verification/1` returns only successful results. A rejection is a `RequestSeal.Adapter.Error` with reason `:request_rejected` in `conn.private[:request_seal].verification`; its `source` is the core `RequestSeal.Error` with the precise reason and layer. Neither contains request bytes or key IDs.

## Clean up

```elixir
:ok = RequestSeal.Custody.Local.release(sender)
Application.delete_env(:webhook_notebook, :signature_policy)
IO.puts("Sender key released")
```

Next: [Phoenix and Plug](../docs/guides/phoenix-and-plug.md) covers trusted proxies, response signing, and the Phoenix endpoint order; the README's webhook example runs the same receiver over real HTTP with Bandit and Req.
